---
// The homepage (docs/plans/website.md §5, laid out as design boards 21–23). Every number here
// comes from DESIGN.md and docs/model-bench.md, with the same caveats: one Mac, one speaker.
// Change them there first. Every claim is true of the app today: no "model inside the app", no
// Esc to cancel, no history retention, no other languages, nothing to download yet.
import Demo from "../components/Demo.astro";
import Mark from "../components/Mark.astro";
import Pill from "../components/Pill.astro";
import { REPO_PUBLIC, REPO_URL } from "../config";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base description="EasyVox is free, open source dictation for the Mac. Hold ⌥Space and talk; when you let go, the text is pasted where your cursor is. The speech model runs on your Mac.">
  <main id="main" class="landing">
    <section class="hero container" aria-labelledby="top">
      <p class="eyebrow">Early development: there's nothing to install yet.</p>
      <Mark height={52} class="hero-mark" />
      <h1 id="top">Hold ⌥Space. <span>Talk. Let go.</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        EasyVox types what you say where your cursor is.
        <span>Free and open source, with the speech model running on your Mac.</span>
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/docs/building">Build from source</a>
        <span class="button button-soon">Download: coming soon</span>
      </div>
      <p class="meta-line">macOS 26 or later <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> Apple silicon <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> MIT license</p>
      <Demo />
    </section>

    <section class="section container" aria-labelledby="how">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 id="how">How it works</h2>
        <p>Nothing to set up after the first run.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li class="step">
          <div class="step-art" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="keycap keycap-option"><span>⌥</span><small>option</small></span>
            <span class="keycap keycap-space"></span>
          </div>
          <p class="step-n">Step 1</p>
          <h3>Hold ⌥Space</h3>
          <p>In any app, wherever your cursor is. A small pill appears near the bottom of the screen.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <div class="step-art" aria-hidden="true">
            <Pill confirmed={["Can", "we", "push", "lunch", "to", "one?"]} />
          </div>
          <p class="step-n">Step 2</p>
          <h3>Talk</h3>
          <p>Your words show up in the pill as you say them. Fainter words may still change.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <div class="step-art" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="textfield"><small><span></span>Messages</small>Can we push lunch to one?<span class="demo-caret"></span></span>
          </div>
          <p class="step-n">Step 3</p>
          <h3>Let go</h3>
          <p>EasyVox finishes the text and pastes it where your cursor is. It stays on the clipboard too.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-flush container" aria-labelledby="features">
      <h2 id="features" class="section-title">Out of the way until you need it.</h2>
      <ul class="features">
        <li>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3.75" y="4.75" width="16.5" height="11.5" rx="1.75"></rect><path d="M2 19.25h20M10 9v3.5M12 8v5.5M14 9.25v3"></path></svg>
          <h3>Runs on your Mac</h3>
          <p>The speech model runs on your Mac, not on a server. No audio or text leaves it.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3.75" y="3.75" width="7" height="7" rx="2"></rect><rect x="13.25" y="3.75" width="7" height="7" rx="2"></rect><rect x="3.75" y="13.25" width="7" height="7" rx="2"></rect><rect x="13.25" y="13.25" width="7" height="7" rx="2"></rect></svg>
          <h3>Works where you type</h3>
          <p>An email, a note, a chat, a commit message: if you can paste into it, EasyVox can type into it.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="2.25" y="7.25" width="19.5" height="9.5" rx="4.75"></rect><path d="M6.25 10.5v3M8.75 9.75v4.5M11.25 10.75v2.5M14 12h4.25"></path></svg>
          <h3>See your words as you speak</h3>
          <p>The pill shows the words as EasyVox hears them, so you know it heard you before you let go.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4.6 13.2A7.5 7.5 0 1 0 6.4 6.9M6.6 3.6l-0.4 3.5 3.5 0.3M12 8.25V12l2.75 2"></path></svg>
          <h3>History stays on this Mac</h3>
          <p>Find something you said earlier and copy it again. History keeps the text, never the audio.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3.25" y="3.25" width="17.5" height="17.5" rx="4.25"></rect><path d="M7.25 9.25h2.6l3.9 5.75h3M13.6 9.25h3.15"></path></svg>
          <h3>Your shortcut</h3>
          <p>Hold ⌥Space, or pick another shortcut in Settings. You can also have it listen while you hold the right Option key alone.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8.25 7.5L3.75 12l4.5 4.5M15.75 7.5l4.5 4.5-4.5 4.5M13.6 5.25l-3.2 13.5"></path></svg>
          <h3>One settings file</h3>
          <p>
            Every setting is in one TOML file, and an edit applies as soon as you save it.
            <a href="/docs/settings">Every key and its default</a>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section container" aria-labelledby="light">
      <h2 id="light" class="section-title">Light on your Mac.</h2>
      <ul class="stats">
        <li>
          <p class="stat">~0 %</p>
          <p>Of the CPU while idle. EasyVox wakes about once every 3 seconds, and uses too little to measure.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="stat">0.65 W</p>
          <p>
            Added to what the Mac draws while you dictate. Drawing the pill's animations on screen adds roughly
            0.15–0.2 W more.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p class="stat">90 MB</p>
          <p>
            Of memory once the model is freed, after 10 minutes without use (about 670 MB with it loaded). Your next
            dictation still starts recording at once.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="caveat">
        Measured in October 2026 on one Mac (an M4 Mac mini), with the default model and one speaker's voice. The
        methods and every number are in the <a href="/docs/model-bench">model bench</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="band" aria-labelledby="private">
      <div class="band-inner container">
        <div class="band-text">
          <h2 id="private">What you say stays on this Mac.</h2>
          <p>
            EasyVox turns your voice into text on this Mac, never on a server. It goes online only to download a speech
            model, and checks the file against its sha256 before using it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="checks">
          <li>
            <h3>No account and no sign-in</h3>
            <p>Allow the microphone and Accessibility, download a model, and start talking.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Online once, for the model</h3>
            <p>After that one download, you can dictate with Wi‑Fi off.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <h3>Text in History, never audio</h3>
            <p>History keeps the text of each dictation, on this Mac. The audio is never saved.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section container" aria-labelledby="models">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 id="models">Two models</h2>
        <p>Pick one in the app. EasyVox downloads it from Hugging Face, once.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="models">
        <li class="model">
          <p class="badge">Default</p>
          <h3>Parakeet TDT 0.6B v2</h3>
          <p>The best all-round model. A 539 MB download.</p>
          <p class="model-credit">
            By NVIDIA, under CC BY 4.0. <a href="https://huggingface.co/nvidia/parakeet-tdt-0.6b-v2">Model card</a>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="model">
          <p class="badge">The small one</p>
          <h3>Whisper base.en</h3>
          <p>For an 8 GB Mac, or when memory matters more than the last bit of accuracy. An 85 MB download.</p>
          <p class="model-credit">
            By OpenAI, under Apache 2.0. <a href="https://huggingface.co/openai/whisper-base.en">Model card</a>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="caveat">
        Both run on <a href="https://github.com/handy-computer/transcribe.cpp">transcribe.cpp</a>, from the team behind
        <a href="https://github.com/cjpais/Handy">Handy</a>, another open source dictation app. The same team makes the
        GGUF builds of both models.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section container faq-section" aria-labelledby="questions">
      <div class="faq-head">
        <h2 id="questions">Questions</h2>
        <p>
          More answers in the <a href="/docs">docs</a>{
            REPO_PUBLIC ? (
              <>
                , or ask on <a href={REPO_URL}>GitHub</a>.
              </>
            ) : (
              "."
            )
          }
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <details open>
          <summary>Is it really free?</summary>
          <p>Yes. EasyVox is free and open source, under the MIT license. There's no paid tier, no trial and no account.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>When can I download it?</summary>
          <p>
            Not yet: a DMG and a Homebrew cask are coming soon. Until then, you can
            <a href="/docs/building">build it from source</a>.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does it need the internet?</summary>
          <p>
            Once, to download the speech model (539 MB for the default). After that you can dictate offline, and your audio
            and text never leave your Mac.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which languages?</summary>
          <p>English, for now. Both models are English-only.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which Macs?</summary>
          <p>A Mac with Apple silicon, on macOS 26 or later. Intel Macs aren't supported.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How do I uninstall it?</summary>
          <p>
            Quit EasyVox from the menu bar and drag it from Applications to the Trash. To remove everything, also delete
            <code>~/Library/Application Support/EasyVox</code> (the models and History) and <code>~/.config/easyvox</code>
            (the settings).
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cta container" aria-labelledby="install">
      <Mark height={44} class="hero-mark" />
      <h2 id="install">Talk instead of typing.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        A DMG and a Homebrew cask are coming soon. For now, you can build EasyVox from source: you need Xcode, cmake and
        Rust.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/docs/building">Build from source</a>
        <span class="button button-soon">Download: coming soon</span>
      </div>
      <div class="requirements">
        <h3 id="requirements">Requirements</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>A Mac with Apple silicon</li>
          <li>macOS 26 or later</li>
          <li>English, for now</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
